				"Overview",
				"Herat is situated south of the Paropamisus Mountains (Selseleh-ye Safēd Kōh) in the fertile valley of the Hari River. An ancient civilization on the [[Silk Road]] between [[West Asia]], [[Central Asia]], [[South Asia]] and [[East Asia]], it is a [[Region|regional]] hub in the country's west. The city has a number of historic sites, particularly the Great Mosque of Herat, the Herat Citadel, the Musalla Complex. During the [[Middle Ages]], Herat became one of the important cities of [[Khorasan]], as it was known as the Pearl of Khorasan. After its conquest by Timur, the city became an important center of intellectual and artistic life in the Islamic world. Under the rule of Shah Rukh and Husayn Bayqara, the city served as the focal point of the Timurid Renaissance, whose grandeur is thought to have matched [[Florence]] of the Italian Renaissance as the center of a cultural rebirth.",
